Title: "Algebra"
Id: "b66194ab-1d9d-4b6f-bf99-d68a98b4cc00"


Text: "Abstract algebra studies sets equipped with operations, asking what can
       be proved from the properties of the operations alone, regardless of what
       the elements are. This chapter climbs a ladder of such structures. Each
       rung adds one requirement to the one below it: a bare set with an
       operation becomes, in turn, a magma, a semigroup, a monoid, and finally a
       group, the central object of elementary algebra."
Id: "02a8aafc-c012-4d2a-92ed-b36b902d05a0"


SectionTitle: "Magmas"
Id: "399425a0-d94d-4548-953d-5dc82ac91e36"


Text: "The most basic structure is a set together with a single binary
       operation, with no further conditions imposed. This is a *magma*.
       Everything that follows is a magma with extra properties, so a magma
       marks the ground floor of the entire chapter."
Id: "b6b6f241-917e-4090-8269-5060f249b80c"


[\magma]
Declares: M ::= (X, *) is \set via X
specifies: * is \binary.operation:on{M}
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" M :<->: x_ is \magma.element:of{M}
  written: "x_? \in M?"
Documented:
. called: "magma"
. description: "A magma is a set $X$ together with a binary operation $*$ on it,
                subject to no further axioms. It is the weakest algebraic
                structure of this chapter, and every later structure is a magma
                with additional properties."
Id: "91ffd40c-72fa-4a93-83f3-80ceb1ae124a"


[\magma.element:of{M ::= (X, *)}]
Declares: x "in" X
when: M is \magma
Enables:
. capability: x_ [*] y_ :=> x_ |M.*| y_
  written: "x_? *? y_?"
Documented:
. called: "element of magma $M?$"
. description: "An element of a magma $M$ is simply a member of its underlying
                set. Naming it as its own notion lets the operation of $M$ be
                written directly between two of its elements as $x * y$."
Id: "d9405849-68a5-4721-9090-42eb0b9775d2"


Text: "A quasigroup is a magma in which the operation can always be undone on
       either side: each of the equations $a * x = b$ and $y * a = b$ has a
       unique solution. This is the abstract form of the demand that division be
       possible, and it is where the study of invertibility begins."
Id: "55b0749b-4e2f-4112-a5be-c20a9879d6a3"


[\quasigroup]
Declares: Q ::= (X, *) is \magma via (X, *)
satisfies:
. forAll: a, b "in" Q
  then:
  . existsUnique: x, y "in" Q
    suchThat:
    . a * x = b
    . y * a = b
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" Q :<->: x_ is \quasigroup.element:of{Q}
  written: "x_? \in Q?"
Documented:
. called: "quasigroup"
. description: "A quasigroup is a magma in which left and right division are
                always possible: for all $a$ and $b$ the equations $a * x = b$
                and $y * a = b$ each have a unique solution. It captures
                invertibility without requiring associativity or an identity."
Id: "49582242-a047-48ae-aced-77654d9a48aa"


[\quasigroup.element:of{Q ::= (X, *)}]
Declares: x is \magma.element:of{Q}
when: Q is \quasigroup
Documented:
. called: "element of quasigroup $Q?$"
Id: "2f5a9f56-286c-4703-a953-316fa32e57dc"


SectionTitle: "Semigroups and monoids"
Id: "57daae70-4d40-44bf-9640-46c26d8a4898"


Text: "Imposing associativity on a magma yields a *semigroup*. Associativity is
       what lets a product of several elements be written without parentheses,
       and it is assumed by nearly every structure in mathematics that is called
       an algebra."
Id: "e077a5db-c372-4759-a880-d74270032560"


[\semigroup]
Declares: S ::= (X, *) is \magma via (X, *)
specifies: * is \(associative)::binary.operation:on{X}
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" S :<->: x_ is \semigroup.element:of{S}
  written: "x_? \in S?"
Documented:
. called: "semigroup"
. description: "A semigroup is a magma whose operation is associative, so that
                $(a * b) * c = a * (b * c)$ for all elements. Associativity
                makes repeated products unambiguous, so one may write
                $a * b * c$ without parentheses."
Id: "6ec2faf5-7d2c-4918-a559-6415b94f2b1f"


[\semigroup.element:of{S ::= (X, *)}]
Declares: x is \magma.element:of{S}
when: S is \semigroup
Documented:
. called: "element of semigroup $S?$"
Id: "5ce95378-5760-4728-b0d2-334848109a32"


Text: "A *monoid* is a semigroup with an identity element -- a distinguished
       element that leaves every element unchanged when combined with it. The
       identity is the algebraic counterpart of the number $1$ under
       multiplication, or $0$ under addition."
Id: "c647adb5-0f98-4f7d-ae6a-8ff2229e3437"


[\monoid]
Declares: M ::= (X, *, e) is \semigroup via (X, *)
specifies: e "in" X
satisfies:
. forAll: x "in" M
  then:
  . x * e = x
  . e * x = x
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" M :<->: x is \monoid.element:of{M}
  written: "x_? \in M?"
Documented:
. called: "monoid"
. description: "A monoid is a semigroup possessing an identity element $e$, one
                that satisfies $x * e = x = e * x$ for every $x$. The identity
                is unique, and it plays the role that $0$ plays for addition or
                $1$ for multiplication."
Id: "034bf6ff-ba6e-4fac-b47e-65cc85d15642"


[\monoid.element:of{M}]
Declares: x is \semigroup.element:of{M}
when: M is \monoid
Documented:
. called: "element of monoid $M?$"
Id: "fee7d2ca-5ebc-48a6-8eed-06d623b5520f"


SectionTitle: "Groups"
Id: "5bc745b6-657d-446f-b5f0-cae8c199112a"


Text: "A *group* is a monoid in which every element has an inverse: for each $x$
       there is a $y$ with $x * y = e$ and $y * x = e$. Groups are the central
       structure of elementary algebra, being exactly the structures whose
       operation can always be undone. They are the abstract theory of symmetry."
Id: "5fb80940-d8de-44bf-8117-eeee203d4755"


[\group]
Declares: G ::= (X, *, e) is \monoid via (X, *, e)
satisfies:
. forAll: x "in" G
  then:
  . exists: y "in" G
    suchThat:
    . x * y = e
    . y * x = e
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" G :<->: x_ is \group.element:of{G}
  written: "x_? \in G?"
Documented:
. called: "group"
. description: "A group is a monoid in which every element $x$ has an inverse
                $y$, so that $x * y = e = y * x$. Equivalently it is an
                associative structure with identity in which every element is
                invertible; this makes it a quasigroup as well as a monoid."
Id: "e4d7371d-29bd-431f-ab09-e114fe89e5cf"


Text: "A group is *finite* when its underlying set has finitely many elements.
       The size of a finite group, its order, governs a great deal of its
       structure, and finiteness is inherited directly from the underlying
       monoid."
Id: "6c628805-5df1-4ad5-b2c3-23bf7dad5acc"


[\(finite)::group]
Refines: G
implicitly:
specifies: G is \(finite)::monoid
Documented:
. adjective: "finite"
Id: "28d2a1c1-89a5-4163-93e3-fbc21c97ec9a"


Text: "The elements of a group inherit the operation of the group, and each also
       carries an inverse. Writing $x^{-1}$ for the inverse of $x$ gives the
       familiar notation used in computations."
Id: "bb323028-2831-4d67-b784-b0918774ab3a"


[\group.element:of{G}]
Declares: x is \monoid.element:of{G}
when: G is \group
Enables:
. capability: x.inv :=> \group.inverse:of{x}:in{G}
  written: "x+?^{-1}"
Documented:
. called: "element of group $G?$"
. description: "An element of a group is a member of its underlying set. Because
                the ambient structure is a group, each such element $x$ has a
                well-defined inverse $x^{-1}$, accessible through its inverse
                operation."
Id: "858abdda-c416-4a4e-a0bf-27ecf84cf008"


[\group.inverse:of{x}:in{G ::= (X, *, e)}]
Defines: y "in" G
when:
. G is \group
. x "in" G
expresses:
. x * y = e
. y * x = e
Documented:
. called: "group inverse of $x?$"
. written: "x+?^{-1}"
. description: "The inverse $x^{-1}$ of an element $x$ of a group is the unique
                element $y$ satisfying $x * y = e = y * x$. Its uniqueness is
                proved below; it is what allows every equation in a group to be
                solved."
Id: "9b52b844-0cfa-4238-8c73-8b38265cca81"


Text: "Two elements of a magma *commute* when the order in which they are
       combined does not affect the result. In a general group most pairs do not
       commute; the ones that do are of particular interest."
Id: "a36041ed-5bdd-4e9c-84ce-719a69b7708b"


[x \.commutes.with:?in{M ::= (X, *)}./ y]
States:
when:
. M is \magma
. x, y "in" M
that: x * y = y * x
Documented:
. called: "$x?$ commutes with $y?$@[M]{ in $M?$}"
. description: "Two elements $x$ and $y$ of a magma commute when $x * y = y *
                x$. An operation in which every pair of elements commutes is
                exactly a commutative one."
Id: "3d7a204c-966d-47e7-a5b4-8f9e20fd749c"


SectionTitle: "Subgroups"
Id: "29c1456f-88a5-4b56-a5b8-57859c40aedc"


Text: "Before defining subgroups we record a small set-theoretic fact that the
       definition needs: forming the Cartesian square of a subset lands inside
       the Cartesian square of the whole set. It lets a subgroup's operation be
       recognized as a restriction of the parent operation."
Id: "7541e6eb-f20a-497b-ae90-65c891609d26"


[\cross.of.subset.is.subset.of.cross]
Theorem:
given:
. X is \set
. Y \:subset:/ X
then:
. (.Y \.set.cross./ Y.) \:subset?:/ (.X \.set.cross./ X.)
Id: "db0160ae-5d28-4ad1-b2b6-d22964a88a2b"


Text: "A *subgroup* is a subset of a group that is itself a group under the
       inherited operation. It must contain the identity, be closed under the
       operation, and contain the inverse of each of its elements. Subgroups are
       the substructures through which the internal architecture of a group is
       studied."
Id: "91933534-107f-44fe-9ad6-4829664f8cd6"


[H ::= (X', *', e') \:subgroup:/ G ::= (X, *, e)]
Declares: H ::= (X', *', e')
when: G is \group
specifies:
. X' \:(nonempty)::subset:/ X
. *' is \binary.operation:on{X'}
. e' "in" X'
satisfies:
. (.*' := \restriction:of{`*`}:on{X' \.set.cross./ X'}.)[:1:]
. have:
  . forAll: x, y "in" H
    then:
    . x *' y "in" H
    . x *' y = x * y
  . forAll: x "in" H
    then: x.inv "in" H
  asserting: H is \group
Documented:
. called: "$H?$ is a subgroup of $G?$"
. written: "H? \leq G?"
. description: "A subgroup $H \leq G$ is a nonempty subset of $G$ that is closed
                under the group operation and under taking inverses, and whose
                operation is the restriction of that of $G$. Under these
                conditions $H$ is again a group in its own right."
Justification:
. [1]
  have: *' := \restriction:of{`*`}:on{X' \.set.cross./ X'}
  asserting: (.X' \.set.cross./ X'.) \:subset?:/ (.G \.set.cross./ G.)
  because: X' \:subset?:/ X
  by: \cross.of.subset.is.subset.of.cross#given{X := X; Y := X'}
Id: "d8fe82d3-6f50-4711-ad64-76d4eb0615ff"


Text: "That a subgroup is genuinely a group is recorded as a theorem, making
       explicit the property the definition was arranged to secure."
Id: "5a7d1bd6-3a8d-4584-bc3d-505eb0ba0680"


[\subgroup.is.group]
Theorem:
given:
. G is \group
. H \:subgroup:/ G
then: H is? \group
Id: "946d3180-8614-4bd2-8fd6-d9bdce721e04"


SectionTitle: "Products and further structure"
Id: "786f4620-5e2a-4871-856e-ae9220f82f4b"


Text: "New groups can be assembled from old ones. The *direct product* of two
       groups operates on ordered pairs, applying each group's operation in its
       own coordinate. It is the simplest way of combining groups, and it builds
       larger groups out of smaller building blocks."
Id: "aa01e2aa-23e6-428f-8555-cabb841d1543"


[G1 ::= (X1, *_1, e1) \.group.direct.product./ G2 ::= (X2, *_2, e2)]
Defines: G3 ::= (X3, *_3, e3) is \group
when: G1, G2 is \group
expresses:
. X3 := {(x1, x2) : x1 "in" X1; x2 "in" X2}
. (a1_, a2_) *_3 (b1_, b2_) := (a1_ *_1 b1_, a2_ *_2 b2_)
. e3 := (e1, e2)
Documented:
. called: "direct product of groups $G1?$ and $G2?$"
. written: "G1? \times G2?"
. description: "The direct product $G_1 \times G_2$ has as elements the ordered
                pairs $(x_1, x_2)$ with $x_i \in G_i$, and multiplies them
                coordinatewise. Its identity is the pair of identities, and it
                is itself a group."
Id: "3a354c63-fc8c-4873-9751-62ecc651a5a9"


Text: "As with subgroups, the fact that the direct product is a group is stated
       as a theorem in its own right."
Id: "16235348-2acd-494b-ae40-6814eab32507"


Theorem:
given: G1, G2 is \group
then: G1 \.group.direct.product./ G2 is? \group
Id: "d65025cf-8bf1-454f-9507-55890ab9f88c"


Text: "A first computation in the group axioms: the inverse of a product is the
       product of the inverses taken in the reverse order. The reversal is
       essential, since the operation need not be commutative."
Id: "a71309b4-a9ab-464d-a885-34cbcf992013"


Theorem:
given:
. G is \group
. x, y "in" G
then: (x * y).inv = y.inv * x.inv
Id: "ef1b2ca8-1e12-4779-8e32-ed5cf8787c68"


Text: "When the operation of a group *is* commutative, the group is called
       *abelian*. Abelian groups are far more tractable than groups in general,
       and they are the setting for much of number theory and linear algebra."
Id: "a608ecec-59d4-4939-a43e-3296c77eff39"


[\(abelian)::group]
Refines: G ::= (X, *, e)
satisfies:
. forAll: x, y "in" G
  then: x * y = y * x
Documented:
. adjective: "abelian"
Id: "1dc777cd-31a5-489a-8b5a-05b94c647fb9"


Text: "The chapter closes with two more consequences of the axioms. The first is
       that inverting an element twice returns the original element."
Id: "6619e809-8d59-4180-820d-ff40b5914fdf"


Theorem:
given:
. G ::= (X, *, e) is \group
. x "in" G
then: (x.inv).inv = x
Id: "8c61fb44-d5ab-4be8-804a-0d6a95d83331"


Text: "The second is the uniqueness of inverses, promised when the inverse was
       defined: any two elements that both act as an inverse of $x$ must be
       equal. Uniqueness is what justifies writing $x^{-1}$ for *the* inverse."
Id: "de8e5629-d42e-4d98-b713-62d92e61d306"


Theorem:
given:
. G ::= (X, *, e) is \group
. x, y1, y2 "in" G
where:
. x * y1 = e
. y1 * x = e
. x * y2 = e
. y2 * x = e
then: y1 = y2
Id: "a27fd487-85b4-4f18-b0ab-4be63db69f43"
